response:
To find the repeating decimal representation of $\frac{3}{11}$, we can perform long division:

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
\multicord{ 3} & 3.000000 & \ldots \\
\cline{2-3}
11.000000 & 1 \\
\multicord{ 11} & 30 \\
\cline{1-2}
\multicord{ -2} & 300 \\
\multicord{ 27} & 30
\cline{2-2}
\multicord{ -3} & 300 \\
\multicord{ 37} & 300
\cline{1-2}
\multicord{ -3} & 300 \\
\multicord{ 30} & 300
\cline{1-2}
\multicord{ -300} & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\]

The quotient is $0.272727\ldots$ and the remainder is $0$. Therefore, the repeating decimal representation is $0.\overline{27}$ or $0.2700000\ldots$. So in this case, we have $a = 2$ and $b = 7$. Thus, the sum $a+b$ is $2+7=\boxed{9}$.

problem:
The graph of the rational function $\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}$ is shown below, with a horizontal asymptote of $y = 0$ and a vertical asymptote of $ x=-1 $. If $q(x)$ is quadratic, $p(2)=1$, and $q(2) = 3$, find $p(x) + q(x).$
[asy]
size(8cm);
import graph;

Label f; 
f.p=fontsize(6); 

real f(real x) {return (x-1)/((x-1)*(x+1));}

int gridsize = 5;
draw((-gridsize,0)--(gridsize,0), black+1bp, Arrows(8));
draw((0,-gridsize)--(0, gridsize), black+1bp, Arrows(8));
label("$x$", (gridsize, 0), E);
label("$y$", (0, gridsize), N);
label("$0$", (0,0),SE, p=fontsize(8pt));
for (int i=-gridsize+1; i<0; ++i){
    label("$"+string(i)+"$",(i,0),S, p=fontsize(8pt));
    label("$"+string(i)+"$",(0,i),E, p=fontsize(8pt));}
for (int i=1; i<=gridsize-1; ++i){
    label("$"+string(i)+"$",(i,0),S, p=fontsize(8pt));
    label("$"+string(i)+"$",(0,i),E, p=fontsize(8pt));}



draw(graph(f,-5,-1.2));
draw(graph(f,-.8,0.85));
draw(graph(f,1.15,5));
draw((-1,-5)--(-1,5), dashed);
draw(circle((1,.5),.15));



[/asy]

